{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbZubFxHLXRAHnc7eau9", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This matches the format and also checks that the month and day are in valid ranges:\n\n```regex\n^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])$\n```\n\n- `\\d{4}` is a four-digit year.\n- `(0[1-9]|1[0-2])` is a month from 01 to 12.\n- `(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])` is a day from 01 to 31.\n- `^` and `$` anchor the pattern to the whole string. Remove them if you're searching for dates inside longer text, and use `\\b` at each end instead.\n\nIt matches `2026-10-01` and rejects `2026-13-01`, `2026-10-32` and `26-10-01`.\n\nThe regex still accepts some dates that don't exist, such as `2026-02-30`, because it doesn't know how many days each month has. If you need that check, parse the string with your language's date library after the regex matches, for example `datetime.date.fromisoformat` in Python. That's simpler and more reliable than a regex that handles leap years.\n\nIf you only need to check the shape of the string, this shorter version is enough:\n\n```regex\n^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$\n```"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7295, "cache_read_input_tokens": 11903, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7295}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "4cf5f528-a6c3-406e-97a2-5339e0310e93", "uuid": "8c0ae2f4-763c-469e-b349-6cba0326647d", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T13:07:13.466Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbZuareeQK6TG1hqBCbT"}
